    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. The board is leaning heavily towards the decentralized approach to mitigate that risk. Can you actually run some predictive simulations on the Q4 supply chain if we were to phase out the central core and move to those 50 localized nodes? I need to see if the trade-offs in \"oversight and synchronization\" are actually worth the resilience gains. Try to be as granular as possible with the potential impact on delivery timelines and cost-per-unit.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Running granular predictive simulations requires specific current data inputs regarding node capacity, latency tolerances, and current operational costs. Generally, the initial phase-out will likely show a slight increase in short-term cost-per-unit due to redundant localized infrastructure and synchronization overhead. However, simulations typically demonstrate a significant decrease in the variance of delivery timelines during simulated disruption events, proving the resilience trade-off is valuable for stability.",
      "source": "target",
